<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ikausu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ikausu was a king of the Philistine city of Ekron in the early 7th century BCE and son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padi">Padi</a>; his name corresponds to the biblical Achish. He appears in Assyrian records among the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>. A dedicatory inscription found at Tel Miqne in 1996, naming him as builder of a temple to the goddess Ptgyh, confirmed the site as Ekron.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ikausu.jpg" alt="Ikausu" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_feigning_madness_before_Achish.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
